   131	## 2. Exact nearby-fibre genus for arbitrary affine singularities
   132	
   133	Choose a reduced equation
   134	
   135	```text
   136	B=f^(-1)(0).                                              (2.1)
   137	```
   138	
   139	The normalization `A1` has one end, so `B` has one normalization place at
   140	infinity and its link `K_infinity` is a knot.  Corrected Neumann--Rudolph
   141	Lemma 7.1 makes `f` good.  Choose a sufficiently small nonzero regular value
   142	`epsilon`, with no other critical value between `0` and `epsilon`.  Goodness
   143	prevents vanishing cycles at infinity, so `f^(-1)(epsilon)` is obtained from
   144	`B` by smoothing precisely its affine singularities and has the same infinity
   145	knot.
   146	
   147	Let `r_p` be the number of local branches at `p`, and let `M_p` be its local
   148	Milnor fibre.  A compact core of the normalization is a disk.  Remove one
   149	small disk around each of the `r_p` normalization preimages of every `p`, then
   150	glue in `M_p`.  Gluing along circles changes no Euler characteristic, and
   151	
   152	```text
   153	chi(M_p)=1-mu_p.
   154	```
   155	
   156	Consequently the compact nearby fibre `F_epsilon` has
   157	
   158	```text
   159	chi(F_epsilon)
   160	 = 1-sum_p r_p + sum_p(1-mu_p)
   161	 = 1-sum_p(mu_p+r_p-1)
   162	 = 1-2 sum_p delta_p.                                    (2.2)
   163	```
   164	
   165	Its only boundary component is `K_infinity`.  Therefore
   166	
   167	```text
   168	g(F_epsilon)=Delta_aff(B).                                (2.3)
   169	```
   170	
   171	Neumann's Theorem 1 makes this compact core the minimal Seifert surface, so
   172	
   173	```text
   174	g_3(K_infinity)=Delta_aff(B).                             (2.4)
   175	```
   176	
   177	Equation (2.3) is the exact generalization of the node computation.  For an
   178	ordinary node, `r=2`, `mu=1`, and `delta=1`; for a unibranch singularity the
   179	same formula detects delta even though there is no two-point normalization
   180	fibre.
