    63	## 2. Constant-unit theorem
    64	
    65	### Proposition 2.1
    66	
    67	If \(q\in\mathbf C[x,y]\) is reduced and nonconstant and \(V(q)\) is
    68	connected, then every unit of \(R=A[s]/(s^2-q)\) is constant.
    69	
    70	### Proof
    71	
    72	Every element of \(R\) has a unique expression \(u=a+bs\), with
    73	\(a,b\in A\). If \(u\) is a unit, multiplication by \(u\) on the free
    74	rank-two \(A\)-module \(R\) is invertible. Its determinant is
    75	\[
    76	 N(u)=a^2-qb^2\in A^*=\mathbf C^*.
    77	\]
    78	Multiplying \(u\) by a constant, possible because \(\mathbf C\) is
    79	algebraically closed, we may suppose \(N(u)=1\). Hence
    80	\[
    81	                  (a-1)(a+1)=q b^2.                 \tag{2.1}
    82	\]
    83	If \(a\) is constant, (2.1) immediately forces \(b=0\), and the proof
    84	is finished. Assume from now on that \(a\) is nonconstant; in particular
    85	\(a-1\) and \(a+1\) are both nonzero.
    86	
    87	For a nonzero polynomial \(f\), call the product of the irreducible
    88	factors occurring to odd exponent its *odd support*. Since \(A\) is a
    89	UFD and \(q\) is squarefree, (2.1) says that the odd support of
    90	\((a-1)(a+1)\) is exactly \(q\). No nonconstant irreducible can divide
    91	both \(a-1\) and \(a+1\). Let \(q_-\) (respectively \(q_+\)) be the
    92	product of the irreducible factors of \(q\) dividing \(a-1\)
    93	(respectively \(a+1\)). Then \(q=q_-q_+\), up to a nonzero constant.
    94	The closed subsets \(V(q_-)\) and \(V(q_+)\) cover \(B\), and they are
    95	disjoint: a common point would satisfy \(a=1\) and \(a=-1\).
    96	Connectedness of \(B\) therefore forces one of them to be empty.
    97	
    98	Thus one of \(a-1,a+1\) has empty odd support and hence, after absorbing
    99	a nonzero constant into a square, is \(r_1^2\). The other differs from
   100	it by the nonzero constant \(2\), so after scaling it has the form
   101	\[
   102	                    r_1^2-c_1,\qquad c_1\in\mathbf C^*,             \tag{2.2}
   103	\]
   104	and its odd support is \(q\).
   105	
   106	Choose \(\lambda_1^2=c_1\). The factors
   107	\(r_1-\lambda_1\) and \(r_1+\lambda_1\) have no common zero, and their
   108	odd supports again partition \(B\) into two disjoint closed subsets.
   109	Connectedness makes one odd support empty. That factor is a constant
   110	times a square, say
   111	\[
   112	                    r_1\mathbin{\pm}\lambda_1=d_1 r_2^2,
   113	                    \qquad d_1\in\mathbf C^*.                       \tag{2.3}
   114	\]
   115	Substitution in the other factor gives, after multiplication by a
   116	constant, \(r_2^2-c_2\) with \(c_2\ne0\) and odd support \(q\). The
   117	same argument iterates.
   118	
   119	If \(r_1\) were nonconstant, total degrees in (2.3) would give
   120	\[
   121	 \deg r_1=2\deg r_2,
   122	 \quad \deg r_2=2\deg r_3,
   123	 \quad\ldots .
   124	\]
   125	Thus the positive integer \(\deg r_1\) would be divisible by \(2^n\)
   126	for every \(n\), a contradiction to the standing assumption that
   127	\(a\), hence \(r_1\), is nonconstant. Therefore only the constant case
   128	can occur; as checked above it has \(b=0\) and \(a=\pm1\) under the
   129	normalization. Undoing the constant scaling gives
   130	\(R^*=\mathbf C^*\). \(\square\)
   131	
   132	### Checks on hypotheses
   133	
   134	- Reducedness is essential in the parity step.
   135	- Connectedness is used exactly to prevent the odd support from splitting
   136	  between the two constant-separated factors.
   137	- No normality or special singularity type is used in Proposition 2.1.
   138	- Nonconstancy excludes the vacuous unbranched case.
