   114	## 2. The one-place Alexander gate
   115	
   116	Let `B` be the irreducible image of (0.1), with `nu` generically one-to-one.
   117	The normalization compactifies from `A1` to `P1` by adding the single point
   118	`t=infinity`.  Hence `B` has one normalization place at infinity and
   119	
   120	```text
   121	K_infinity = B intersect S_R^3                              (2.1)
   122	```
   123	
   124	is a knot for all sufficiently large transverse spheres.
   125	
   126	Neumann's link-at-infinity construction represents `K_infinity` by a toral
   127	(Eisenbud--Neumann graph-link) splice diagram in `S3`.  Eisenbud--Neumann
   128	Theorem 12.1 applies to graph links in integral homology spheres, hence in
   129	particular to this knot in `S3`.  In its one-component clause it gives, up to
   130	a Laurent unit,
   131	
   132	```text
   133	Delta_K(t)
   134	 = (t-1) product_v (t^(ell_v)-1)^(delta_v-2),          (2.2)
   135	```
   136	
   137	where `v` runs over non-arrowhead vertices, `delta_v` is the valency, and
   138	`ell_v` is the linking number of the knot with the corresponding virtual
   139	component.  Formula (2.2) is an identity in the Laurent fraction field; its
   140	apparent denominator factors cancel because the left side is the knot
   141	Alexander polynomial.
   142	
   143	For one component every relevant `ell_v` is nonzero.  Replacing a negative
   144	`ell_v` by its absolute value only changes a Laurent unit.  Consequently all
   145	numerator and denominator factors in (2.2) are of the form `t^N-1`.  After
   146	cancellation, every nonzero zero of `Delta_K` is still a zero of some
   147	`t^N-1`.  Therefore
   148	
   149	```text
   150	one-place polynomial plane-curve knot at infinity
   151	  ==> every Alexander zero is a root of unity.          (2.3)
   152	```
   153	
   154	Here and below an Alexander polynomial is an element of `Z[t,t^(-1)]`
   155	defined up to multiplication by a unit `+/-t^j`.  Such a unit has no zero in
   156	`C*`, so (2.3) and the off-unit-circle obstruction are normalization
   157	independent.  The displayed choice in (0.2) has `Delta_beta(1)=1`; this fixes
   158	the sign but the Laurent shift remains immaterial.
   159	
   160	This implication is the entire global theorem consumed below.  Equivalently,
   161	it follows by decomposing a graph knot into torus-knot, cabling, and
   162	connected-sum pieces and applying the standard cable and product formulas,
   163	but (2.2) avoids any convention about signed cabling.
   164	
   165	Now take a generic finite linear projection of `B`.  Over a large circle in
   166	the projection base, its sheets form a closed braid isotopic to (2.1).  The
   167	product of the based finite braid-monodromy factors is that boundary braid,
   168	up to inversion from the orientation convention and up to conjugacy from a
   169	change of the boundary basepoint.  Inversion replaces the Alexander
   170	polynomial by its reciprocal up to a unit, while conjugacy does not change
   171	the closed braid.  Thus the root-of-unity gate (2.3) applies without fixing
   172	those conventions.
