   243	## 4. Why the entire Hurwitz orbit is excluded
   244	
   245	A Hurwitz move on adjacent factors has the form
   246	
   247	```text
   248	(g_i,g_(i+1))
   249	  -> (g_i g_(i+1) g_i^(-1), g_i)                       (4.1)
   250	```
   251	
   252	or its inverse, depending on convention.  It preserves the ordered product.
   253	A simultaneous conjugation of all factors conjugates that product.  Hence a
   254	Hurwitz-equivalent factorization of (3.1) has boundary braid equal or
   255	conjugate to (3.2); its closure and Alexander polynomial are unchanged.
   256	
   257	Changing the orientation convention replaces `beta` by `beta^(-1)`.  Its
   258	Alexander polynomial is reciprocal to (0.2) up to a unit, so the real zero
   259	`r` is replaced by `r^(-1)`, again off the unit circle.  None of these
   260	choices escapes (2.3).
   261	
   262	It follows that rearranging the same vanishing paths, choosing a different
   263	distinguished basis, or using an equivalent quasipositive factorization
   264	cannot turn the charged packet into a polynomial one-place curve.  A viable
   265	primitive escape must change the boundary braid type, not merely its
   266	factorization.
