   329	## 6. Scope, tangencies, and unibranch singularities
   330	
   331	The no-go uses only the total boundary knot.  Therefore it remains valid if
   332	the affine interpretation of the factors is deformed while the boundary
   333	braid stays in the conjugacy class of (3.2).  In particular, relabeling the
   334	two conductor preimages or changing local coordinates at the node cannot
   335	help.
   336	
   337	It does not license either of the following extrapolations.
   338	
   339	1. A tangential two-point conductor generally replaces a node full twist by
   340	   a higher positive pure band.  That changes the total braid and requires a
   341	   fresh Alexander calculation.
   342	2. A unibranch affine singularity contributes its own positive local braid.
   343	   It can change both the splice data and the infinity knot even when the
   344	   normalization remains `A1` and the total conductor census stays small.
   345	
   346	Accordingly this report does not claim that every one-node polynomial `A1`
   347	curve has trefoil infinity, that every such complement is cyclic, or that
   348	all admissible transposition colorings preserve a perfect matching.  Those
   349	would require an additional semigroup/splice classification.  The ruling
   350	rows `(A1,Q=1)` and `(P1,Q=0)` are also not consumed by the Alexander gate.
   351	
   352	What is proved is exactly
   353	
   354	```text
   355	the frozen primitive packet, and every Hurwitz-equivalent packet,
   356	is not the complete braid monodromy of a one-place polynomial A1 curve. (6.1)
   357	```
