    80	Mathematische Annalen 278 (1987), 409--439,
    81	DOI 10.1007/BF01458078;
    82	with "Corrigendum: Unfoldings in knot theory",
    83	Mathematische Annalen 282 (1988), 349--351,
    84	DOI 10.1007/BF01456981; corrected Lemma 7.1.
    85	
    86	Horst Schubert,
    87	"Knoten und Vollringe",
    88	Acta Mathematica 90 (1953), 131--286,
    89	DOI 10.1007/BF02392437.
    90	```
    91	
    92	The corrigendum is essential: it corrects the definition of a good
    93	polynomial and explicitly restates and reproves the knot-at-infinity lemma.
    94	No false implication from quasipositivity to strong quasipositivity is used.
    95	
    96	## 2. The infinity knot is a trefoil
    97	
    98	Choose a reduced polynomial equation
    99	
   100	```text
   101	B=f^(-1)(0).                                             (2.1)
   102	```
   103	
   104	Because the normalization is `A1`, its compactification adds one point.
   105	Thus `B` has one normalization place at infinity and its link at infinity
   106	`K_infinity` is a knot.  Corrected Neumann--Rudolph Lemma 7.1 says that a
   107	reduced fibre with knot link at infinity makes `f` good: every fibre is
   108	regular at infinity.
   109	
   110	Choose a small disk about zero containing no other critical value and a
   111	nonzero regular value `epsilon` in it.  Goodness identifies the links at
   112	infinity of `f^(-1)(0)` and `f^(-1)(epsilon)` and prevents vanishing cycles
   113	at infinity.  On the complement of a Milnor ball around the sole node,
   114	Ehresmann triviality shows that the nearby fibre changes only by the local
   115	node smoothing.
   116	
   117	A compact core of the normalization is a disk.  Delete small disks around
   118	the two node preimages; the Euler characteristic becomes
   119	
   120	```text
   121	1-2=-1.                                                  (2.2)
   122	```
   123	
   124	The ordinary-node Milnor fibre is an annulus, of Euler characteristic zero.
   125	Gluing it to the two new boundary circles leaves Euler characteristic `-1`
   126	and leaves only the boundary `K_infinity`.  The nearby regular fibre core
   127	therefore has genus one.
   128	
   129	Neumann's regular-curve theorem identifies this core with the unique minimal
   130	Seifert surface of `K_infinity`.  Hence
   131	
   132	```text
   133	g_3(K_infinity)=1.                                       (2.3)
   134	```
   135	
   136	The link at infinity of an affine algebraic plane curve is a toral graph
   137	link.  A graph knot in `S3` is generated from the unknot by connected sums
   138	and cabling.  Schubert's formulas
   139	
   140	```text
   141	g(J#K)=g(J)+g(K),
   142	g(C_(p,q)(J))=p g(J)+(p-1)(|q|-1)/2                    (2.4)
   143	```
   144	
   145	show that the only genus-one graph knot is `T(2,+/-3)`, equivalently the
   146	right or left trefoil.  Therefore
   147	
   148	```text
   149	K_infinity = trefoil or mirror trefoil.                 (2.5)
   150	```
   151	
   152	## 3. The infinity group surjects onto the affine group
   153	
   154	This step is independent of the number `d` in (0.2).  Choose any generic
   155	finite projection of `B`, with regular-fibre meridians
   156	`x_1,...,x_N`, and based finite braid monodromies
   157	`beta_1,...,beta_s`.  Zariski--van Kampen gives
   158	
   159	```text
   160	G_aff=pi1(A2-B)
   161	 = F_N / <<x_j^(-1) beta_v(x_j): all j,v>>.             (3.1)
   162	```
   163	
   164	Let `beta_infinity=beta_1...beta_s`, up to the harmless inverse convention.
   165	The Artin presentation of the closed boundary braid gives
   166	
   167	```text
   168	G_infinity=pi1(S3-K_infinity)
   169	 = F_N / <<x_j^(-1) beta_infinity(x_j): all j>>.        (3.2)
   170	```
   171	
   172	The individual relations in (3.1) imply the product relation in (3.2).
   173	Consequently the identity on fibre meridians induces a meridian-preserving
   174	surjection
   175	
   176	```text
   177	G_infinity ->> G_aff.                                   (3.3)
   178	```
   179	
   180	Composing (3.3) with (0.2) preserves the image.  Thus the trefoil group has
   181	a transitive degree-`d` representation in which every meridian maps to a
   182	transposition.
   183	
   184	## 4. Trefoil meridional rank bounds the degree
   185	
   186	Either trefoil orientation has the two-meridian presentation
   187	
   188	```text
   189	G_3_1=<a,b | aba=bab>.                                  (4.1)
   190	```
   191	
   192	Put `A=rho(a)` and `B=rho(b)`.  Both are transpositions in `S_d`.  There are
   193	only three possibilities.
   194	
   195	1. If `A=B`, their image is `C2` and their nontrivial orbit has size two.
   196	2. If `A` and `B` are distinct and disjoint, they commute, so
   197	   `ABA=B` and `BAB=A`; relation (4.1) would force `A=B`.  This case is
   198	   impossible.
   199	3. If `A` and `B` are distinct and overlap in one letter, they satisfy (4.1)
   200	   and generate `S3` on exactly three letters.
   201	
   202	Hence every meridian-transposition image of the trefoil group has
   203	
   204	```text
   205	largest orbit <=3,
   206	image order in {2,6}.                                   (4.2)
   207	```
   208	
   209	It cannot be transitive on `d>=4` letters.  This contradicts (0.2) and proves
   210	(0.3).
   211	
   212	Equivalently, a transitive graph of transposition generators on `d` letters
   213	needs at least `d-1` edges, whereas the trefoil group has meridional rank two.
   214	The direct relation analysis above is slightly sharper because it also
   215	classifies the two-generator images.
   216	
   217	## 5. Scope and campaign consequence
   218	
   219	The theorem applies simultaneously to every projection degree and every
   220	based braid/Hurwitz presentation of a branch satisfying (0.1).  No Alexander
   221	calculation is necessary once the exact affine singularity census is known.
   222	In the plane-Jacobian campaign it excludes the irreducible ordinary-node
   223	control for every connected transposition cover of degree at least four, not
   224	only the frozen quartic packet.
   225	
   226	The following remain outside its scope.
   227	
   228	1. A tangential two-point conductor has delta invariant greater than one;
   229	   its nearby regular fibre has higher genus.
   230	2. A point-bijective unibranch singularity contributes additional delta even
   231	   though it is invisible to the two-point-fibre count.
   232	3. A reducible connected source forest is not normalized by one `A1`; node
   233	   smoothing can connect components without creating the same genus.
   234	4. Additional branch components give the affine monodromy image additional
   235	   meridional generators.
   236	5. Non-transposition generic inertia and disconnected covers do not satisfy
   237	   (0.2).
   238	
   239	The theorem therefore closes an all-degree **irreducible, sole ordinary-node,
   240	one-place, connected simple-cover lane**, not the full charged
   241	`R4-CYCLE-0` horn.  The proper-block ruling data are not used.
   242	
   243	## 6. Deterministic replay and firewalls
   244	
   245	```text
   246	a6b000430e872e372c6c4cad9d694baa522746717eeef837e52884b206eff6fa
   247	  ops/block_descent_one_node_all_degree_meridional_rank_replay.py
   248	```
   249	
   250	Ordinary, `python3 -O`, and `python3 -OO` executions are byte-identical, with
   251	
   252	```text
   253	stdout SHA-256:
   254	583da5d653ad234c9d642c3c7cc8d8ad31b30ab61f417c12df8fbdb7fa32831d
   255	
   256	status=PASS-ONE-NODE-ALL-DEGREE-MERIDIONAL-RANK
   257	payload_sha256=c4593ac35230962ea90bc535534fb9fa77abe0e409d18158743b660a0f1c1cd1
   258	```
   259	
   260	The replay exhausts all ordered transposition pairs satisfying the trefoil
   261	relation in `S_d` for `2<=d<=10`, verifies the symbolic equal/overlap/disjoint
   262	census, finds maximum orbit size three and maximum image order six, and
   263	checks the exact formulas
   264	
   265	```text
   266	equal pairs:                d(d-1)/2;
   267	overlapping distinct pairs: d(d-1)(d-2).                (6.1)
   268	```
   269	
   270	The mutation `--mutate-allow-degree-three` exits nonzero because the standard
   271	`S3` trefoil coloring is transitive in degree three.  This is a group-level
   272	control only; it does not assert `(2,2)` inertia in degree three.
   273	
   274	The replay does not encode the corrected Neumann--Rudolph lemma, local node
   275	smoothing, Neumann's minimal-Seifert theorem, graph-knot classification,
   276	Schubert's genus formula, Zariski--van Kampen, the closed-braid Artin
   277	presentation, or the boundary-to-affine quotient.  Those are the written
   278	theorem interfaces above.
   279	
   280	Nothing here constructs a finite cover or Keller map, resolves a tangential,
   281	unibranch, or reducible horn, proves JC2, or touches the formalization tree,
   282	top ledgers, sealed lanes, or the campaign pilot log.
   283	
   284	<!-- BODY-END -->
   285	
   286	## Seal
   287	
   288	- Body definition: every byte through the unique standalone `<!-- BODY-END -->` line,
   289	  including its terminating newline; this seal is outside the body.
