  4989	        <div class="bbl-abstract"><p>
  4990	<p>Con&#173;sid&#173;er&#173;ing that the study of plane cu&#173;ves has an over 2000 year his&#173;tory and is the seed from which mod&#173;ern al&#173;geb&#173;ra&#173;ic geo&#173;metry grew, sur&#173;pris&#173;ingly little is known about the to&#173;po&#173;logy of af&#173;fine al&#173;geb&#173;ra&#173;ic plane curves. We to&#173;po&#173;lo&#173;gic&#173;ally clas&#173;si&#173;fy “reg&#173;u&#173;lar” al&#173;geb&#173;ra&#173;ic plane curves in com&#173;plex af&#173;fine 2-space us&#173;ing “splice dia&#173;grams:” cer&#173;tain dec&#173;or&#173;ated trees that code Puiseux data at in&#173;fin&#173;ity. (The reg&#173;u&#173;lar&#173;ity con&#173;di&#173;tion — that the curve be a “typ&#173;ic&#173;al” fiber of its de&#173;fin&#173;ing poly&#173;no&#173;mi&#173;al — can con&#173;jec&#173;tur&#173;ally be avoided.) We also show that the splice dia&#173;gram de&#173;term&#173;ines such al&#173;geb&#173;ra&#173;ic in&#173;form&#173;a&#173;tion as the min&#173;im&#173;al de&#173;gree of the curve, even in the ir&#173;reg&#173;u&#173;lar case. Among oth&#173;er things, this en&#173;ables al&#173;geb&#173;ra&#173;ic clas&#173;si&#173;fic&#173;a&#173;tion of reg&#173;u&#173;lar al&#173;geb&#173;ra&#173;ic plane curves with giv&#173;en to&#173;po&#173;logy.</p>
